# KNOWN GAP (todo/t0401) — THE DURABLE REPRO THAT ITEM NEVER HAD.
#
# `v.push(^f)` where `f` is a `Callable` local: `gg check` rc 0, then
# `gg build` rc 101:
#
#   Tier 2a consume-site violation: 1 violation(s) in module '<unknown>'.
#   First: fn @main bb0 i7
#          — CollectionMutator(Vector__Callable__GorgetClosure__push, arg #1)
#          — untracked source consumed (ownership not decided).
#
# `^f` is the EXPLICITLY LEGAL spelling at a consuming position, so the
# compiler crashing on it means the sanctioned remedy does not compile.
#
# ⭐ THE DISCRIMINATOR THE ITEM DID NOT HAVE, and it is not what the item says.
# `t0401` records the crash as "identifier-specific" — closure LITERAL push
# works, identifier push does not. That is half the rule. Measured at
# 7bf11017e, what decides it is HOW THE LOCAL WAS BOUND:
#
#   Callable[int()] c = (): base + 1     # closure LITERAL   -> build rc 101
#   Callable[int()] c = mk(40)           # call RESULT       -> build rc 0
#
# Same syntax at the push, same type, opposite verdicts. So the source's
# `LocalOwnership` state is the axis, not the expression's syntactic class —
# which is why a re-measurement that happened to bind from a helper concluded
# the item no longer reproduced. This fixture binds from a LITERAL deliberately.
#
# ⚠ This is `t1236`'s census question in miniature: the panic SITE is shared
# with `t0938` and `todo/t1226`, the consume-site CLASS is not
# (`CollectionMutator` here, `EnumInit` there, `StructInit(<tuple>)` in t1226),
# and the classification differs too ("untracked" vs "borrowed"). Record the
# class, never the panic site.
#
# INTENDED: builds and runs, printing `8`.

void main():
    int base = 7
    Callable[int()] c = (): base + 1
    Vector[Callable[int()]] v = []
    v.push(^c)
    print(v.get(0).unwrap()())
